is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The outage is caused by the BGP routing subsystem on router1. Evidence: router1’s FRR service is down (systemctl status frr: Active: failed), and its routing table contains only directly connected routes (show ip route: 193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24, no learned routes). Consequently, when pc1 (195.11.14.2) sends traffic to 200.1.1.2, router1 has no route toward 200.1.1.0/24 and drops the traffic. On the peer side, router2’s FRR is running and BGP is configured and advertising 200.1.1.0/24 (router bgp 2 … network 200.1.1.0/24). Router2’s “show ip bgp summary” shows the neighbor 193.10.11.1 stuck in Active with 0 prefixes received, which is consistent with the peer (router1) not running BGP at all. This localizes the failure to router1’s bgpd/FRRouting service, not router2.

Root cause: FRRouting (bgpd) on router1 has crashed or been stopped, leaving no eBGP session to router2 and no route to 200.1.1.0/24.

Corrective fix: Do not restart router2. Restart FRR on router1 (systemctl restart frr). After bgpd comes up, ensure router1 has proper BGP configuration (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, and network 195.11.14.0/24). Verify the BGP session establishes and both routers learn each other’s LAN prefixes. Review /var/log/frr/frr.log on router1 to determine why FRR failed and ensure it is enabled to start automatically. 
=== END ===